MSettings menu structure: 

 "Settings:"

    1 "Preset"  (active Preset)
    2 "Bank"    (active bank)
    3 "Global"

    1.1 "Name"                                                          // blink underscore as cursor and use "value" rotary encoder input to scroll through all 20 character positions. pressing the encoder button enters letter scrolling mode for that positoin. Use "value" encoder to cycle through letters, numbers and special characters. Pressing the encoder button again saves the letter and moves the cursor to the next position. If the user scrolls past the last character position, it should loop back to the first position. If the user scrolls back before the first character position, it should loop to the last position.
    1.2 "Chx Programm"                                                  // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all available midi programs for the selected midi device. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected program and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last program, it should loop back to the first program. If the user scrolls back before the first program, it should loop to the last program.
    1.3 "ChxProgramm"                                                   // same as 1.2 but for the second midi device specified in the struct
    1.4 "Chx Programm"                                          // same as 1.2 but for the third midi device specified in the struct    
    1.5 (contunie if more midi devices are specified in the struct)
    1.6 "Relay 1"                                                       // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all available relay functions. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected function and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last function, it should loop back to the first function. If the user scrolls back before the first function, it should loop to the last function.
    1.7 "Relay 2"                                                       // same as 1.6 but for the second relay specified in the struct                                          

    2.1 "Name"                                                          // blink underscore as cursor and use "value" rotary encoder input to scroll through all 20 character positions. pressing the encoder button enters letter scrolling mode for that positoin. Use "value" encoder to cycle through letters, numbers and special characters. Pressing the encoder button again saves the letter and moves the cursor to the next position. If the user scrolls past the last character position, it should loop back to the first position. If the user scrolls back before the first character position, it should loop to the last position.
    2.2 "Special Functions Button"   
    2.1.1 "Name"                                                        // blink underscore as cursor and use "value" rotary encoder input to scroll through 4 character positions. pressing the encoder button enters letter scrolling mode for that positoin. Use "value" encoder to cycle through letters, numbers and special characters. Pressing the encoder button again saves the letter and moves the cursor to the next position. If the user scrolls past the last character position, it should loop back to the first position. If the user scrolls back before the first character position, it should loop to the last position.
    2.2.1 "Channel"                                                     // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all available midi channels (1-16). Pressing the encoder button saves the selected channel and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past channel 16, it should loop back to channel 1. If the user scrolls back before channel 1, it should loop to channel 16.
    2.2.2 "Active Value"                                                // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all possible values for the selected function. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected value and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum value, it should loop back to the minimum value. If the user scrolls back before the minimum value, it should loop to the maximum value.
    2.2.3 "Inactive Value"                                              // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all possible values for the selected function. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected value and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum value, it should loop back to the minimum value. If the user scrolls back before the minimum value, it should loop to the maximum value.
    
    3.1 "Devices"                                                       
    3.2 "Startup Time"                                                  // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through a range of possible startup times (e.g. 0-60 seconds). Pressing the encoder button saves the selected time and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum time, it should loop back to the minimum time. If the user scrolls back before the minimum time, it should loop to the maximum time.
    3.3 "Screensaver"                                                   // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through available screensaver timeout value in minutes. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected option and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last option, it should loop back to the first option. If the user scrolls back before the first option, it should loop to the last option.
    3.4 "Brightness"                                                    // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through brightness levels (e.g. 1-10). Pressing the encoder button saves the selected level and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum level, it should loop back to the minimum level. If the user scrolls back before the minimum level, it should loop to the maximum level. 

    3.1.1 "Add Device                                                   // pressing the encoder button adds a new device to the struct and creates a new menu item for it under "Devices". The new device should be initialized with default values (e.g. name: "New", midi channel: 1, preset number: 1, CC active value: 127, CC bypass value: 0, bypass preset: 1, mute preset: 1). If the user scrolls past the maximum number of devices allowed (e.g. 8), it should loop back to the first device. If the user scrolls back before the first device, it should loop to the last device.
    3.1.2 "Device 1"                                                    // pressing the encoder button enters the next submenu item level for that device, where the user can edit the device's settings such as name, midi channel, preset number, CC active value, CC bypass value, bypass preset and mute preset. If the user scrolls past the last device, it should loop back to the first device. If the user scrolls back before the first device, it should loop to the last device.
    3.1.3 "Device 2"                                                    // same as 3.1.2 but for the second device specified in the struct
    3.1.4 "Device 3"                                                    // same as 3.1.2 but for the third device specified in the struct                                                


    3.1.x.1 "Name"                                                      // blink underscore as cursor and use "value" rotary encoder input to scroll through 4 character positions. pressing the encoder button enters letter scrolling mode for that positoin. Use "value" encoder to cycle through letters, numbers and special characters. Pressing the encoder button again saves the letter and moves the cursor to the next position. If the user scrolls past the last character position, it should loop back to the first position. If the user scrolls back before the first character position, it should loop to the last position.
    3.1.x.2 "Midi Channel"                                              // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through all available midi channels (1-16). Pressing the encoder button saves the selected channel and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past channel 16, it should loop back to channel 1. If the user scrolls back before channel 1, it should loop to channel 16.
    3.1.x.3 "Preset Number"                                             // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the number of available presets (0-127) for the selected midi device. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected preset and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last preset, it should loop back to the first preset. If the user scrolls back before the first preset, it should loop to the last preset.
    3.1.x.4 "CC active / bypass"                                        // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the CC to toggle "Active" and "Bypass" mode for the selected midi device. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected CC and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum CC number (127), it should loop back to CC 0. If the user scrolls back before CC 0, it should loop to CC 127. 
    3.1.x.5 "Active Value"                                              // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the value for the active state of the selected midi device. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected value and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum value, it should loop back to the minimum value. If the user scrolls back before the minimum value, it should loop to the maximum value.  
    3.1.x.6 "Bypass Value"                                              // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the value for the bypass state of the selected midi device. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected value and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the maximum value, it should loop back to the minimum value. If the user scrolls back before the minimum value, it should loop to the maximum value.
    3.1.x.7 "Bypass Preset"                                             // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the preset number (0-127) that will be activated when the selected midi device is in bypass mode. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected preset and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last preset, it should loop back to the first preset. If the user scrolls back before the first preset, it should loop to the last preset.
    3.1.x.8 "Mute Preset"                                               // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to set the preset number (0-127) that will be activated when the selected midi device is muted. Pressing the encoder button saves the selected preset and exits the menu item. If the user scrolls past the last preset, it should loop back to the first preset. If the user scrolls back before the first preset, it should loop to the last preset.
    3.1.x.9 "Delete Device"                                             // pressing the encoder button deletes the device from the struct and removes its menu item from the "Devices" submenu. If the user scrolls past the last device, it should loop back to the first device. If the user scrolls back before the first device, it should loop to the last device.
    3.1.x.9.1 "Confirm Delete"                                          // blink value and let user use the "value" rotary encoder to scroll through options "Yes" and "No". Pressing the encoder button on "Yes" confirms the deletion of the device, while pressing it on "No" cancels the deletion and returns to the previous menu item. If the user scrolls past "No", it should loop back to "Yes". If the user scrolls back before "Yes", it should loop to "No".